validation=f'''<section class="evidence-ledger architecture-section" id="validation"><p class="research-eyebrow">RESEARCH DEVELOPMENT / WHAT CHANGED + WHAT IS VERIFIED</p><h2>The objections<br><em>became the next question.</em></h2><div class="architecture-grid"><article><span>DAO / START → CHALLENGE → FOLLOW-UP</span><h3>Reweighting is only the start.</h3><p>Sharvina’s initial line of inquiry was quadratic voting. The preprint compared outcomes with observed votes held fixed. In editorial feedback on a rejected submission, an editor questioned the novelty of changed outcomes and noted that capital concentration could respond to new voting rules.</p><p>That objection prompted the Sybil follow-up: what happens when one balance is split among identities? The equal-split model isolates one response; it still does not model endogenous concentration or actual voter behaviour.</p><a href="https://github.com/thetarocks/dao-sybil-resilience">Follow the analytical extension ↗</a></article><article><span>JoSAA / MOTIVATION → MODEL → BOUNDARY</span><h3>Fair in which round?</h3><p>Living in India amid the JEE admissions race made Sharvina curious about counselling fairness. The project connects matching theory to explicit quota rules and Freeze, Float and Slide decisions.</p><p>The distinction that matters: single-round DSIC is established theory; it does not settle the consequences of an irreversible action when later vacancies appear. The synthetic audit explores that narrower question, without claiming to validate official JoSAA outcomes.</p><a href="artifacts/josaa-technical-note.pdf">Read method, results &amp; limitations ↗</a></article><article><span>VALIDATION / EVIDENCE STATUS</span><h3>Preprint ≠ peer review.</h3><p><strong>Public:</strong> SSRN working paper, source histories, benchmark JSON and automated checks. The hosted technical notes are project documentation.</p><p><strong>Author-reported:</strong> editorial feedback and rejection, paraphrased here from private correspondence. The correspondence is not published for independent verification; the feedback is not an endorsement.</p><p>No independently verified user study, external replication or peer-reviewed acceptance is presented. Internal tests support reproducibility, not external validation.</p><a href="{repo}/actions/workflows/validate.yml">Inspect internal CI ↗</a></article></div></section>'''
contributions=f'''<section class="architecture-section" id="contributions"><p class="research-eyebrow">CONTRIBUTION RECORD / ORIGINAL WORK + CODING ASSISTANCE</p><h2>My questions.<br><em>A visible development record.</em></h2><div class="architecture-grid"><article><h3>Research by Sharvina</h3><p>I researched and wrote the DAO preprint and pursued the Sybil follow-up after editorial criticism. For JoSAA, I developed the original matching and multi-round audit project to explore counselling fairness. The original repository history records the engine, quota, data and audit work.</p><a href="{repo}/commits/main/">JoSAA development history ↗</a></article><article><h3>Assisted engineering</h3><p>Codex developed the six educational browser finance models, their explanations and tests, the newer allocation teaching interface and invariant inspector, and this static portfolio. It also repaired the dynamic audit and assignment-state bug, corrected audit counts, and generated the current benchmark notes and evidence.</p><p>Those additions are disclosed here so the site does not imply I independently coded every component or personally discovered every implementation issue.</p></article><article><h3>Analysis &amp; writing boundaries</h3><p>The SSRN preprint is my research writing. The current site copy, maths/code guide and project-hosted technical notes were prepared with AI assistance. Published audit results are tied to inspectable code and synthetic inputs; they are empirical checks within stated assumptions, not new mathematical proofs.</p><a href="{repo}/blob/main/docs/contributions.md">Full source and contribution map ↗</a></article></div></section>'''

