about=f'''<div style="background:#f4f1e9;color:#172b24;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;min-height:100vh;line-height:1.8">{header}<div style="max-width:1180px;margin:auto;padding:40px 5% 70px"><p class="research-eyebrow">ABOUT ME / A RESEARCH PORTFOLIO</p><h1 style="font:500 clamp(43px,6vw,78px)/1.06 Arial,sans-serif;letter-spacing:-3px;margin:25px 0 40px">Learning by<br><em style="color:#285b42">making rules run.</em></h1><div style="max-width:760px;font-size:17px;line-height:1.95;color:#485b4b"><p>I’m Sharvina Srivastava, a high school student interested in computer science, economics and finance. I got drawn to mechanism design through its parallels with real-life politics: how rules shape representation, access and influence. The JEE admissions race around me also made me curious about how fair counselling rules are. Models gave me a way to make those questions concrete.</p><p>That is the thread between these projects. An admissions rule affects access to a scarce seat. A voting rule changes whose voice carries weight. A market’s matching rule determines the price someone actually pays. I want to make those rules easier to inspect and question.</p><p>My starting point in DAO governance was quadratic voting. Editorial feedback on a rejected submission challenged the assumption that votes and capital concentration would stay fixed under a different rule. That led me to the Sybil follow-up. It taught me to ask how participants might respond to the mechanism, beyond recalculating its immediate outcome.</p><p>Next, I’d like to learn from students and DAO participants about where the models miss their experience. I also want to test richer preferences, archive the governance ballots and add identity costs to the Sybil model. Those are next steps, not completed user studies.</p></div><div class="artifact-links">{chip('Projects','/projects/')}{chip('Finance notebook',lab+'/experiments/')}{chip('DAO preprint on SSRN','https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=7103878')}{chip('Source code',repo)}</div><section class="architecture-section" style="margin-top:55px"><p class="research-eyebrow">HOW TO READ THE WORK</p><h2>The model comes<br><em>with its boundaries.</em></h2><div class="architecture-grid"><div><h3>JoSAA</h3><p>Synthetic preferences derived from a sample cutoff table. The single-round result is established theory; the portfolio studies implementation and multi-round counterfactuals.</p></div><div><h3>DAO governance</h3><p>A public SSRN preprint anchors the governance counterfactual. The equal-split Sybil preview is a separate analytical model.</p></div><div><h3>Finance notebook</h3><p>Six established mechanisms, made interactive with seeded inputs, readable explanations, numerical checks and exports.</p></div></div></section></div>{contributions}{validation}{footer}</div>'''
